abstract: |
  Delhi's winter air is usually blamed on three things that arrive together: stagnant weather, smoke from paddy-residue fires in Punjab and Haryana, and Diwali fireworks. We separate them in an exploratory analysis of hourly data from {{ n.n_sites }} monitoring sites in {{ n.n_cities }} Indian cities ({{ n.period_start[:4] }}–{{ n.period_end[:4] }}), NASA VIIRS fire detections and ERA5 weather. Gradient-boosted models remove the effect of weather and, for Delhi, of farm-fire smoke carried by the wind; what remains on Diwali night is the festival's own contribution. Diwali's lunar date moved between 19 October and 14 November over the decade, and Delhi's firecracker rules changed four times, which lets us compare like with like. Three findings stand out. First, the reference monitor at the US Embassy stops reporting during the worst hours of Diwali night: {{ n.cens_diwali|fmt(0, pct=True) }} of late-night Diwali hours are missing, against {{ n.cens_ordinary|fmt(0, pct=True) }} on ordinary autumn nights, so averages built on it understate the festival. Second, using the less-censored CPCB network median, Diwali night adds {{ n.diwali_main_min|fmt }}–{{ n.diwali_main_max|fmt }} µg/m³ above the weather-and-fire baseline in every year observed, {{ n.diwali_above_p99 }} of {{ n.diwali_main_n }} nights beyond the 99th percentile of ordinary nights, and years with a full ban were not lower than years with green crackers. Third, VIIRS detections in Punjab and Haryana fell {{ n.viirs_drop_2021_2025|fmt(0, pct=True) }} between 2021 and 2025, but the share caught by the 1:30 am pass rose from {{ n.night_share_2016_2021|fmt(1, pct=True) }} to {{ n.night_share_2025|fmt(1, pct=True) }}, and in 2025 each detected fire went with a roughly tenfold larger rise in Delhi's weather-adjusted PM2.5, though with wide uncertainty. Both are consistent with burning that has moved out of the satellite's daytime view. We publish a locked, checksummed forecast for Diwali 2026 and release all code, derived data and an interactive companion site.
