## Weather and the winter gap

{% if n.gap_n_sites %}The daily weather models reach a median cross-validated R² of {{ n.daily_r2_median|fmt(2) }} on log PM2.5 across {{ n.daily_models }} sites (interquartile range {{ n.daily_r2_q1|fmt(2) }}–{{ n.daily_r2_q3|fmt(2) }}; {{ n.daily_r2_us_delhi|fmt(2) }} at the US Embassy). We leave out of the comparison the {{ n.gap_n_sites_below_r2 }} sites below R² = {{ n.gap_min_r2 }}, mostly short records in Punjab and Haryana where holding out whole years leaves the model little to learn from. Across the remaining {{ n.gap_n_sites }} sites in {{ n.gap_n_cities }} cities, weather accounts for a median of {{ n.gap_weather_share_delhi_ncr|fmt(0, pct=True) }} of the winter-minus-monsoon gap in Delhi-NCR ({{ n.gap_n_cities_delhi_ncr }} cities, range {{ n.gap_weather_share_delhi_ncr_range[0]|fmt(0, pct=True) }}–{{ n.gap_weather_share_delhi_ncr_range[1]|fmt(0, pct=True) }}), {{ n.gap_weather_share_source|fmt(0, pct=True) }} in Punjab and Haryana ({{ n.gap_n_cities_source }} cities, {{ n.gap_weather_share_source_range[0]|fmt(0, pct=True) }}–{{ n.gap_weather_share_source_range[1]|fmt(0, pct=True) }}), {{ n.gap_weather_share_igp|fmt(0, pct=True) }} in the rest of the Gangetic Plain ({{ n.gap_n_cities_igp }} cities) and {{ n.gap_weather_share_rest|fmt(0, pct=True) }} outside the plain ({{ n.gap_n_cities_rest }} cities; Fig. 4). The burning belt stands out: where paddy residue is burned, weather explains only about half of the winter rise, and in Delhi-NCR, downwind of it, about three-fifths. Further east along the plain and in southern and western cities, most of the seasonal swing is the monsoon washing the air clean and the shallow winter boundary layer trapping what is emitted all year. Removing weather shrinks the north–south difference in winter PM2.5 from {{ n.gap_ns_gap_obs|fmt }} to {{ n.gap_ns_gap_norm|fmt }} µg/m³, so about {{ n.gap_ns_gap_weather_share|fmt(0, pct=True) }} of that difference is weather and the rest is emissions.{% endif %}

{% if n.trend_step_pct is defined %}Did the 2020 drop last? In Delhi, largely yes (Figs. 3 and 5). Over the whole record the weather-normalised series at the US Embassy falls by {{ (-n.trend_pct_per_year)|fmt(1) }}% a year (Theil–Sen), but the decline is not smooth: the only change point PELT finds in the deseasonalised series is at {{ n.trend_change_points|join(", ") }}. During the national lockdown, normalised PM2.5 was {{ (-n.trend_lockdown_pct)|fmt(0) }}% below its 2016–2019 level. From June 2020 to September 2026 it averaged {{ (-n.trend_step_pct)|fmt(0) }}% below that level (one-sided Mann–Whitney p < 0.001), and within that later period it fell by only {{ (-n.trend_post_slope_pct)|fmt(1) }}% a year (95% CI {{ (-n.trend_post_slope_ci[1])|fmt(1) }} to {{ (-n.trend_post_slope_ci[0])|fmt(1) }}). The lower level that followed the lockdown held, but little further progress followed it. Because the normalisation model includes a smooth time trend, it may spread part of a sharp step over neighbouring months; the step and the slope should be read together.{% endif %}

