## Diwali

In every year with data, Diwali night rises far above the weather-and-fire baseline (Fig. 6). In the CPCB network median the excess ranged from {{ n.diwali_main_min|fmt }} to {{ n.diwali_main_max|fmt }} µg/m³ (mean {{ n.diwali_main_mean|fmt }}), against a 99th percentile of {{ n.diwali_placebo_p99|fmt }} µg/m³ across {{ n.diwali_placebo_n|fmt }} ordinary nights; {{ n.diwali_above_p99 }} of {{ n.diwali_main_n }} Diwali nights exceeded it (Fig. 7).

The firecracker rules made no visible difference. The mean excess was {{ n.diwali_permissive_mean|fmt }} µg/m³ in the two years without a bursting restriction ({{ n.diwali_permissive_years|join(", ") }}), {{ n.diwali_green_window_mean|fmt }} µg/m³ in green-cracker years ({{ n.diwali_green_window_years|join(", ") }}) and {{ n.diwali_full_ban_mean|fmt }} µg/m³ in the full-ban years the network covers ({{ n.diwali_full_ban_years|join(", ") }}){% if n.diwali_kruskal_p is not none %} (Kruskal–Wallis p = {{ n.diwali_kruskal_p|fmt(2) }}){% endif %}. The samples are small and the archive gap removes three full-ban Diwalis, so this is descriptive, but nothing in the record suggests the bans shrank the spike. The smallest excess ({{ n.diwali_2017|fmt }} µg/m³) came in 2017, when the Supreme Court suspended sales three weeks before an early Diwali. The two largest came in 2021, under a full ban ({{ n.diwali_2021|fmt }}), and 2025, the first year green crackers were allowed again ({{ n.diwali_2025|fmt }}), which also had the fewest farm fires of the decade.

The farm-fire term matters little on Diwali night. Including it in the baseline changed the US Embassy Diwali excess by a median of {{ n.fire_term_median_change|fmt(0) }} µg/m³, and with or without it the excess is unrelated to the fire exposure on Diwali night (Spearman ρ = {{ n.diwali_fire_rho_fire|fmt(2) }} and {{ n.diwali_fire_rho_nofire|fmt(2) }}, n = {{ n.cens_n }}, neither significant). The festival's excess is local; it is not farm smoke being misattributed.

{% if n.fp and n.fp.ratio %}The Diwali evening carries a firework fingerprint at CPCB Delhi sites (Fig. 10). Against the same hours a week earlier, the PM2.5/PM10 ratio rose in {{ n.fp.ratio.share_higher|fmt(0, pct=True) }} of {{ n.fp.ratio.n }} site-years (median {{ n.fp.ratio.median_ref|fmt(2) }} → {{ n.fp.ratio.median_diwali|fmt(2) }}, Wilcoxon p < 0.001), so the extra particles are fine ones{% if n.fp.so2 %}, and SO₂, released by the sulphur in fireworks, rose in {{ n.fp.so2.share_higher|fmt(0, pct=True) }} (median {{ n.fp.so2.median_pct_change|fmt(0, sign=True) }}%, p < 0.001){% endif %}. The traffic gases moved the other way{% if n.fp.no2 %}: NO₂ changed by a median of {{ n.fp.no2.median_pct_change|fmt(0, sign=True) }}%{% endif %}{% if n.fp.co %} and CO by {{ n.fp.co.median_pct_change|fmt(0, sign=True) }}%{% endif %}, consistent with fewer vehicles on the road on Diwali evening. A rise in fine particles and SO₂ without a rise in NO₂ and CO points to fireworks rather than traffic or a general build-up.{% endif %}

The timing follows local custom (Fig. 9). In Delhi the median excess peaks around midnight and stays high until morning. In Mumbai and Hyderabad it peaks between 8 and 11 pm on Diwali. In Chennai the largest peak comes on the evening before Diwali as dated in Delhi, consistent with Deepavali being celebrated on Naraka Chaturdashi in Tamil Nadu; Kolkata, with few years of data, shows peaks on both evenings.

