## The fire-count paradox

VIIRS detections in Punjab and Haryana during October–November peaked at {{ n.viirs_peak|fmt }} in {{ n.viirs_peak_season }} and fell to {{ n.viirs_2025|fmt }} in 2025, {{ n.viirs_drop_2021_2025|fmt(0, pct=True) }} below 2021 (Fig. 11). The night pass tells a different story. Before 2024 the 1:30 am pass caught {{ n.night_share_2016_2021|fmt(1, pct=True) }} of detections on average; in 2024 it caught {{ n.night_share_2024|fmt(1, pct=True) }} and in 2025 {{ n.night_share_2025|fmt(1, pct=True) }}. In Punjab, night-pass detections reached {{ n.night_punjab[2024] }} in 2024 and {{ n.night_punjab[2025] }} in 2025, against at most {{ n.night_punjab_2016_2023_max }} in any season from 2016 to 2023, even though daytime detections fell by more than 90%. The rise survives removal of fixed heat sources. A satellite that only looks twice a day cannot show when fires start, but more fires still burning at 1:30 am is what a shift of ignition into the evening would produce, and it agrees with geostationary evidence reported elsewhere [@iforest2025; @nasaeo2025].

Delhi's weather-adjusted PM2.5 responds to the fire index over one to four days rather than on a single day: Spearman ρ is {{ n.lag_rho[0]|fmt(2) }} on the same day and between {{ [n.lag_rho[1], n.lag_rho[2], n.lag_rho[3], n.lag_rho[4]]|min|fmt(2) }} and {{ [n.lag_rho[1], n.lag_rho[2], n.lag_rho[3], n.lag_rho[4]]|max|fmt(2) }} at lags of one to four days. Per unit of the index, the response was small and fairly stable in 2016–2021 (mean {{ n.coupling_early|fmt(1) }}% per 100 index units) and about ten times larger in 2025 ({{ n.coupling_2025|fmt(0) }}%, 95% CI {{ n.coupling_2025_lo|fmt(0) }} to {{ n.coupling_2025_hi|fmt(0) }}%; Fig. 12). The 2025 interval is wide and only just excludes zero, and 2024 is too uncertain to read, so this is suggestive rather than conclusive. It is, however, what an undercount would produce: if each detected fire stands for more burning than before, each detection goes with more smoke. This offers a second explanation for the weak coupling between counts and Delhi's air reported by @mangaraj2025, alongside the local controls they discuss. To make the estimate robust to how the data are split, the residuals behind it average predictions over five random cross-fitting splits.

{% if n.evening %}The ground test at {{ n.evening.n_early + n.evening.n_late }} site-seasons in Punjab and Haryana does not show the shift (Fig. 13): the harvest-to-December evening ratio had a median of {{ n.evening.early_median|fmt(2) }} before 2022 and {{ n.evening.late_median|fmt(2) }} in {{ n.evening.late_seasons|join(", ") }} (one-sided Mann–Whitney p = {{ n.evening.mannwhitney_p_late_greater|fmt(2) }}). With the archive gap, the later period rests on few seasons, and 2025 had so little burning that a change in its timing would barely move urban monitors.{% else %}The ground test at Punjab and Haryana monitors could not be completed: the archive gap leaves too few later seasons with both harvest and December data (Fig. 13).{% endif %}

